/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A process is reversible if the system and its surroundings can both be returned to their exact starting states, leaving no change anywhere.
   - Two conditions must hold at the same time.
   - The first condition removes finite gradients inside and across the boundary.
   - p is the pressure at the moving boundary in kPa, dV is the small volume change in m³, and W is the work in kJ.
   - A reversible process creates no entropy, so it is the yardstick for everything else.
   - Every real process carries at least one of these.
   - Entropy and the Clausius inequality turn these signatures into a number.
   - Internal reversibility concerns only what happens inside the boundary: the gas has no pressure gradient, no temperature gradient and no internal friction, so at every instant it is uniform and in equilibrium.
   - External reversibility concerns the link with the surroundings: heat crosses only to a reservoir at the same temperature, and work is delivered to a load that is raised with no outside friction.
   - No real process is reversible.
*/
