/* book exam traps: Quasi-static is necessary but not sufficient. A slow process with friction, or with a finite temperature difference across the wall, is still irreversible. | Reversible gives maximum work out of an expansion and minimum work into a compression. Do not apply the maximum rule to a compressor. | Free expansion gives W = 0 but is not reversible. For an ideal gas in an insulated flask the temperature also stays the same, which fools students into calling it isothermal and reversible. | Internally reversible is weaker than reversible. The boundary may still lose work to outside friction. | The entropy of the system may fall in an irreversible process, as when a hot block cools. The second law is applied to system plus surroundings together. */
