/* book exam traps: Temperatures must be in kelvin. Using °C in 1−T₂/T₁ gives a meaningless number. Convert first. | Efficiency is a ratio of energies per cycle. It does not depend on m or on the cycle's size; do not multiply η by mass. | Q₂ in 1−Q₂/Q₁ is the positive heat rejected. If your first-law sign convention makes Q₂ negative, do not subtract a negative by mistake. | An efficiency above 1−T₂/T₁ is impossible for any engine between those reservoirs. Equality means reversible; a real engine is lower. | The Carnot cycle is not a real Rankine or Brayton cycle. Those cycles come later in this subject; do not use their practical efficiencies as Carnot limits. */
